gs so here we go we've  split it based on the presence of a  comma we can provide something like the  expand  parameter with a value of true and here  we have a brand new data frame you can  see we've split it by street name city  state and zip code it really was as as  easy as that and then of course we can  choose which columns we want to keep  choose which which ones we want to  remove  etc all of that is possible with pens  that's really all i wanted to show you  in tonight's stream i know it was a  quick whirlwind tour of running through  all of these different functionalities  that we can do with text  in pandas whether it be changing the  casing lower casing upper casing fixing  capital letters removing white space not  replacing characters splitting um  there's all kinds of functionalities and  we've only really scratched the surface  so hopefully it wasn't too crazy or too  hectic i know for people who are brand  new to this um there's a lot of syntax  to explore you're also simultaneously  learning a programming language in this  case python sort of simultaneously so it  can be a difficult journey but i hope it  at a bare minimum that it's given you a  sense of what is possible and that's the  most important thing here that you have  a sense of what you can do with pandas  the syntax and the understanding and the  configuration will come with practice  and with time all right trust me on that  so with that i do want to again remind  you if you're curious about learning  more about what pandas can do for you  you can pick up pandas in action it's  available on manning's la  meep program which is its early release  program you can then get the book in its  early format provide feedback and  helpful commentary to help improve it  before its publication and then you can  also pick up the type the actual  book  when it comes out you'll get the final  copy  when it is actually published all right  if you're interested in more of a visual  learning style i also have a 19 hour  pandas course available on udemy.com  it's a lot slower a lot more slowed down  with a lot more examples we have a lot  more  time in 20 or 19 hours  to cover more material than we do right  here in the hourly stream but  irrespective of which one you choose or  if you choose both i think they're both  helpful resources if you're picking up  this powerful data analysis toolkit that  continues to grow in popularity and  reach with every passing month so  hopefully this has been helpful and  beneficial for you and uh best of luck  in all of your coding endeavors see you  soon
 

